    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm studying for the USMLE and I'm a bit stuck on the RAAS pathway. Can you explain the mechanism of action for ACE inhibitors, like lisinopril? Specifically, how do they interact with the system to lower blood pressure?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "ACE inhibitors, like lisinopril, work by blocking the Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE). This enzyme is responsible for converting Angiotensin I into the powerful vasoconstrictor, Angiotensin II. By inhibiting this conversion, ACE inhibitors decrease Angiotensin II levels, leading to vasodilation and thus lowering blood pressure. This reduction in vasoconstriction dramatically helps in managing hypertension.",
      "source": "target",
