    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you go a bit deeper into the downstream effects? Like, specifically how does the decrease in Angiotensin II affect aldosterone levels and sodium/water retention? I want to make sure I have the whole chain down for the exam.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lowering Angiotensin II also significantly decreases the stimulation of aldosterone release from the adrenal cortex. Aldosterone normally promotes sodium and water retention by acting on the kidneys. With less aldosterone, there is decreased sodium and water reabsorption, leading to increased excretion (natriuresis and diuresis). This reduction in extracellular fluid volume further contributes to the overall decrease in blood pressure.",
      "source": "target",
