    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Got it. So basically it's a cascade of blocking one thing to stop the next. For my notes, can you summarize the whole sequence from the original stimulus of renin all the way to the drop in blood pressure? Just a quick step-by-step breakdown would be great.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The RAAS cascade begins when renin is released, converting angiotensinogen to Angiotensin I. ACE then converts Angiotensin I into the potent vasoconstrictor Angiotensin II. ACE inhibitors block this step, drastically reducing Angiotensin II and subsequently lowering aldosterone. This cascade ultimately results in reduced vasoconstriction and decreased sodium/water retention, leading to lower blood pressure.",
      "source": "target",
